/** @page inference Scores, gradients and Hessians

@tableofcontents

- [Bivariate copulas](#inference-bicop)
- [Vine copulas](#inference-vinecop)
- [Reusing intermediate quantities](#inference-reuse)
- [Per-observation parameters](#inference-per-row)

Both `Bicop` and `Vinecop` expose the derivatives of the log-likelihood with
respect to the parameters. These are the ingredients for standard errors,
Wald tests, sandwich covariance estimates and gradient-based optimization, and
they are available for parametric families only.

@section inference-bicop Bivariate copulas

- `scores(u)` — an \f$ n \times p \f$ matrix of per-observation scores,
  \f$ \partial \log c(u_i; \theta) / \partial \theta \f$.
- `gradient(u)` — the summed score, i.e. the gradient of the log-likelihood.
- `hessian(u)` — the \f$ p \times p \f$ Hessian of the log-likelihood.
- `hessian_full(u)` — the per-observation Hessians, not summed.
- `scores_cov(u)` — \f$ \sum_i s_i s_i^\top \f$, the outer-product ("BHHH")
  covariance of the scores.
- `scores_full(u)` — the scores together with the intermediate quantities.

At a maximum-likelihood fit the gradient is numerically zero, which is the
cheapest available check that a fit converged.

@snippet inference.cpp bicop

@section inference-vinecop Vine copulas

The vine versions take the same names. The columns of `scores()`, and hence the
entries of `gradient()` and the rows and columns of `hessian()`, are ordered
`(tree, edge, parameter)`: tree 0's edges from left to right, and within an edge
the pair copula's own parameters in order.

The `step_wise` flag selects which derivative you get:

- `step_wise = true` (the default) gives the score of the **step-wise** MLE. Each
  pair copula's gradient treats its pseudo-observations as fixed, which is what
  the sequential fitting procedure actually optimizes.
- `step_wise = false` gives the gradient of the **full** log-likelihood,
  propagating through the h-function cascade.

The distinction is not cosmetic: at a fitted model the step-wise gradient
vanishes while the full gradient generally does not, because the sequential
procedure is not a joint maximizer.

@snippet inference.cpp vinecop

@section inference-reuse Reusing intermediate quantities

The derivative cascade is the expensive part. `scores_full()` returns the
per-edge densities, h-functions and their derivatives alongside the scores, and
`hessian_full()` returns the per-edge Hessian blocks rather than assembling them,
so a caller that needs several of these pays for the cascade once.

@snippet inference.cpp reuse

@section inference-per-row Per-observation parameters

`pdf()`, `pdf_full()`, `scores()`, `scores_full()`, `gradient()` and `hessian()`
also accept an \f$ n \times p \f$ matrix holding one full-vine parameter vector
per observation, with columns in the same `(tree, edge, parameter)` order.
Broadcasting a single vector across the rows reproduces the fixed-parameter
results exactly; varying the rows is what makes covariate-dependent parameters
possible. Continuous, all-parametric models only.

@snippet inference.cpp per-row

*/
